my name is mariana ignatesco i work in  one of the two legal units of digit  trade i'm a case handler oh much better  [Music]  my name is colton brown i'm the head of  unit of one of the two legal units of dg  trade  my name is martin lucas i'm the head of  the legal affairs unit in digit trade  [Music]  the european commission has launched a  new complaint system for reporting  market access barriers and breaches of  trade and sustainable development  commitments in the eu trade agreement  the single entry point  was launched  in  2020 so the commission launched a single  entry point and what is the single entry  point the single entry point is there  for companies when they face issues with  market access in third country markets  so we have systems in place to deal with  those market access barriers it's a  one-stop shop  for  companies from the eu but also business  association or civil society or even any  citizen to launch a complaint and why  would a complaint be launched so either  a complaint is launched because there is  a market access issue so there is a  barrier either behind the border or at  the border  or um that there is a problem an  infringement of what we call the trade  and system development provisions  so the binding rules that we negotiate  on labor environment climate and many  other issues and then obviously if  things go really wrong then we may  consider also to launch what we call the  desperate settlement  [Music]  the eu stands for  rules-based  trade  and rules are only as good as their  enforcement  dispute settlement  is basically holding everyone to what  they have committed to  it's about trust in relations  dispute settlement should not be seen as  a hostile act  it is actually a tool that keeps  everyone honest dispute settlement is  a legal process  where  states can resolve the differences now  the most important dispute settlement  framework is in the world trade  organization the wto  where the eu is one of the main users of  dispute settlement  but we also have  those procedures in our free trade  agreements  and they are being used more and more in  our free trade agreements we have  what  i think it makes sense of what we call a  fork in the road clause  so you go down one way you can't come  back so you either choose the wto or you  choose the free trade agreement you  can't do both at the same time and the  way we look at this is we analyze what  are the obligations that we're having  that we think the third country is not  respecting and that we want to to to  push and so what really drives the issue  is are there additional commitments that  we've negotiated  that are not being respected that we  want to to pursue  via a bilateral dispute and if you look  at the examples of the cases that we've  taken most of them not all of them but  most of them are  the bilateral disputes are ones that we  could only have taken under  the bilateral free trade agreement so  the korea case there are no labour  commitments  in the world trade organization so we  couldn't have brought the case  [Music]  the first step is to gather  information so to gather the relevant  legislation to  gather information on the economic  impact and other considerations a big  part of the work is translation of the  documents because it's usually disputes  against third countries and you need to  translate those documents from  those languages after that we  start working on a legal analysis we  look into what possible  legal provisions of either the  bilateral agreement or wto agreements  are are being infringed and then  we also discuss with member states and  we explain the problem and what we  intend to do  and after all the the green lights we  uh launch  the dispute  when we look at the record of of the  cases that we have we have brought we  have a very high success rate  of winning our offensive  offensive cases  and we very rarely need to resort to the  very final enforcement  tool that we have and that is to impose  trade sanctions  [Music]  we make a difference when  we bring about change on the ground  yeah  um  the korea example career ratified three  of the four we still work in the fourth  one but ratified three of the four  international labor organization  conventions that amended its trade union  act most rewarding is  talking to the companies  trying to understand the problem that  they are having and at the end you know  launching a dispute uh to address that  problem  and of course when you get a  you know a positive result uh a panel  report in your favor  you feel like you've made a difference  when you can see whether whether it be  trade unions or whether it is people who  export goods  export services when you see that they  have a problem resolved  you can you can go home happy you've  done your job  [Music]
 

